<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piura</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as San Miguel de Piura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piura is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, near the Pacific coast. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> in 1532 as San Miguel, it was the first Spanish city in Peru, set up before his march to meet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>. Moved several times, it settled on its present site in 1588 and is now the capital of the Piura Region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piura.jpg" alt="Piura" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Josué Quevedo Macalupú · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PLAZA_DE_ARMAS_DE_PIURA_-_PIURA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Piura lies in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, north of the Sechura Desert along the Piura River, about 981 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and near the border with Ecuador. It is the capital of the Piura Region and Piura Province, and its population was 484,475 in 2017. The origin of the name is uncertain. One proposal derives it from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> word meaning a supply deposit or granary, while other scholars connect it with the Sechura-Catacao languages and a suffix linked to water. The name first appears in 1535 for the river valley, not for a settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory was inhabited by native groups called tallanes and yungas, and later the Muchik culture took control and merged into the Vicús culture. The Chimor, Moche and Wari Empire occupied the area around the city, and Tupac Inca Yupanqui of the Inca Empire ruled it for at least forty years before the Spanish arrived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> founded San Miguel de la Nueva Castilla at the Tallán settlement of Tangarará in July or August 1532, the third Spanish city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Two years later the inhabitants moved to a second settlement near the Monte de los Padres estate, where chroniclers report 23 encomenderos and where they stayed more than forty years.</p><p>Around 1578 most inhabitants moved to the port of Paita, discouraged by the harsh climate. Thomas Cavendish, an English pirate, raided and ruined the city between January and May 1587, seizing a large amount of loot, and the inhabitants then moved to their fourth and final site at the head of the Catacaos Valley, beside the pre-Columbian Tacalá Dam. In the viceroyalty Piura was an obligatory stop on the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, since each viceroy landed at Paita and travelled through Piura overland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Piura declared its independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 4 January 1821, a few months before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> did on 28 July 1821. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Piura was created in 1861 with Piura as its capital. In the 1860s the cultivation of Pima cotton, brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> by Emilio Hilbck, spurred industry and drew many immigrants. The naval hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-grau-seminario">Miguel Grau Seminario</a> was born in Piura on 27 July 1834, though since 2011 the Peruvian state has given Paita as his official birthplace. Piura is known as the city of eternal heat because it is hot all year round, and it is the commercial and administrative centre of its department.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last independent king, Minchançaman, pushed the frontier north at least to <b>Piura</b> and possibly to Tumbes, and south almost to the Lima valley, giving Chimor some 1,000 kilometres of coastline and about two-thirds of the farmland of the desert coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its heartland lay in the Moche and Chicama valleys near modern Trujillo, and its art and temples eventually reached from the <b>Piura</b> valley in the north to the Nepeña valley in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/moche">Moche · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He founded <b>San Miguel de Piura</b> in 1532 and the City of the Kings, Lima, in 1535.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-castile">Governorate of New Castile</a></li></ul></section>
